            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

There are a few key locations where excessive air leakage exists in your home. The largest one appears to be air leakage coming up through the heating ductwork, and I think my pictures in the crawlspace show that some of the heat ducts have fallen off their connections - this would explain your very high heating bills !! Other air leakage locations include the current 3" chimney for the hot water tank (that sometimes blows out the pilot light) and around the attic hatch. There was also some leakage coming from around the built in shelves in the centre of the house.



I will email you the "garbage bag test" so you can determine exactly which heating ducts around the house have very little heat flow. You should also have all the heating ductwork in the crawlspace investigated. As that ductwork travels through an "unheated" crawlspace, all the ductwork should be fully insulated &amp; all of the heat supply pipe connections should be in place and properly sealed &amp; insulated.



I would expect the potential air leakage reduction opportunities in this house would provide a 17% - 25% reduction in your heating bills. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a 17% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 167&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;5.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. If you achieve a 25% reduction in air leakage, the annual savings will increase &amp; the rebate would increase to $600.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a second home energy evaluation, where the air leakage reduction will be measured. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of both the original house and the front addition are 2x6 construction with an estimated R20 batt insulation. As discussed for your future planning, I have modeled adding an addition R10 breathable exterior rigid insulation. It is recommended to have the exterior rigid insulation represent at least 50% of the R-value of the insulation located within the stud wall.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls up to a minimum R30 insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 279&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;9.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



</mainWalls>
                <windows>

Overall, I think the current windows are in reasonable shape. For your future planning, I have modeled an upgrade for all windows to ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows.





&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing the windows with ENERGY STAR® qualified units rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 135&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;4.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</windows>
                <doors />
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house has a 40 USG GSW propane hot water tank with a continuous pilot light and it vents up a 3" chimney - this tank has an estimated energy factor of 0.55, which means about 45% of the energy going into this tank is either wasted up the chimney or given off in excess heat into the house (which isn't great in summer). I would recommend you consider replacing this older tank with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 172&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;8.1 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil furnace is older and has previously been modified from a chimney vent to a direct vent design (I am not familiar with this conversion so cannot comment on the code compliance of this conversion). As the furnace is still older &amp; was originally labeled as 82.1% Annual Fuel Utilization Efficiency (AFUE), I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat. This furnace will draw its' combustion air directly from outside, and vent combustion out through a sidewall pipe - so no chimney is required.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 777&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;16.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;., and you would qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------









</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely with the short and dark days of winter. Although winter may provide little or no usable energy (depending on your latitude in the NWT), the long hours of sunlight in spring, summer and fall can provide significant solar heating and electricity generation opportunities for up to eight months of the year.  As Solar Photovoltaic (PV) system prices continue to drop, they become more competitive with the cost of grid-supplied and especially off-grid generation.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a 3 kW solar photovoltaic system on the rear SW facing roof of your house, flush mounted on the roof, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save about $ 836&lt;/b&gt; per year in electricity costs, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 5,000 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate, you must apply and receive pre-approval. The grant amount differs according to the purchase cost of the system: 1/3 of purchase cost, up to a maximum of $5,000 in a diesel electricity community. Applicants are encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program&lt;/b&gt;





-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Crawlspace Issues&lt;/b&gt;



While looking in the crawlspace, there appeared to be some significant issues in there:



1. The floor above the crawlspace is fully insulated, which usually means the crawlspace is deemed "unheated" space, however I see water pipes running through the crawlspace &amp; there is some heating ductwork that is uninsulated. I am hoping those water pipes are just for summer "outside water" taps &amp; they are closed &amp; drained during winter?

2. When I look to the right, I see an opening off the end of the heating ductwork and a pipe laying on the ground beside it. I think one of the heat ducts has fallen off, and your heating system is primarily pushing heat into the crawlspace rather than into the house. This is likely why your heating bills are so high.

3. There was some insulation lying on the ground &amp; I'm not sure what it is from.

4. I will email you the "garbage bag" test so you can check the flow from each of the heating ducts entering the house - there may be various rooms where the heat ducts have come apart?



Ideally, you would like the heat to be coming into the house &amp; not the crawlspace. You will therefore need to have someone fully explore under the house to ensure that all heating ducts are connected, sealed and insulated. There should be no water pipes in the crawlspace that are exposed to cold temperatures in the winter. There should be no openings into the insulation into the floor above the crawlspace - if there are any holes, they need to be sealed. This is not a fun job, however required if you want to significantly reduce your heating bills &amp; increase your comfort in the house.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Ventilation&lt;/b&gt;



Your home currently has the following ventilation:



1. The kitchen range hood recirculates inside the house.

2. A bathroom fan that vents to the outside, however does not operate very well.

3. The clothes dryer vents to the outside, however also has very little air flow.



You should consider replacing the bath fan and the clothes dryer with ENERGY STAR® qualified unis and ensure they are both vented to the outside, and perhaps choose a bathroom fan with an integrated humidity sensing "instant on" feature ....... or have it installed with a timer switch (5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so the fan can ensure all the excess humidity from showers is expelled &amp; then automatically turn off so heat in the house is not wasted.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Depressurization Test&lt;/b&gt;



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the kitchen range hood fan recirculates internally however the bathroom fan and dryer both vent to the outside - however they are both not drawing alot of air ??)), indicated a pressure drop of 1.1 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment should not cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current hot water tank does vent up a chimney, however that would be eliminated if you complete the recommended hot water heater upgrade.



During my assessment of your home, there were some older smoke detectors, however I did not see any carbon monoxide (CO) detectors in the house. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Fort Simpson&lt;/b&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Simpson:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.31 per kWh up to the TPSP threshold, $0.70 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $1.07 per litre 

Propane - $0.60 per litre

Wood - $367.50 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $7.35 per bag, $393.75 per tonne bulk delivered



